{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-116024",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/a76856ea0a6b50b821ba6c39e6a87a5acf5aabd704454b4875540b3970f3d249.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are negative everywhere.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{2y-{y}^{2}-1}=-\\frac{1}{(y-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{\u2032}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{\u2032}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 1.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is negative for all $y\\ne 1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}=-\\frac{y+1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to \u2213\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{3}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for $y>1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
